WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.043 --> 00:00:08.460
My name is Aaron Williams, and
I'm a senior data scientist

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:10.220
at the Urban Institute.

00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:12.610
I make fake data for a living.

00:00:12.610 --> 00:00:14.360
In some scientific circles,

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:16.690
this would be a huge
scandal, but for me, it's

00:00:16.690 --> 00:00:19.590
an opportunity to
further science.

00:00:19.590 --> 00:00:20.850
Administrative data.

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:23.230
These are data that are
collected for reasons entirely

00:00:23.230 --> 00:00:26.160
other than research, like
administering the unemployment

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:28.860
insurance system or
enforcing the tax code,

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:30.810
are incredibly
useful for research,

00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:36.140
but understandably have huge
confidentiality concerns.

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:37.480
I work at the Urban Institute.

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:40.130
We're a collection of
economists, sociologists,

00:00:40.130 --> 00:00:43.670
criminologists, urban
planners and data scientists

00:00:43.670 --> 00:00:46.460
committed to improving the world
through social and economic

00:00:46.460 --> 00:00:48.170
policy research.

00:00:48.170 --> 00:00:50.390
We would do a lot to
access administrative data,

00:00:50.390 --> 00:00:53.390
and we do do a lot to
access administrative data.

00:00:53.390 --> 00:00:55.670
But we understand there's
a fundamental tension

00:00:55.670 --> 00:00:58.730
between accessing these
data and the confidentiality

00:00:58.730 --> 00:01:00.675
of the data.

00:01:00.675 --> 00:01:02.050
That's why at the
Urban Institute

00:01:02.050 --> 00:01:05.500
we've been working with the IRS
to create fully synthetic tax

00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:06.930
data.

00:01:06.930 --> 00:01:09.150
Synthetic data
are fake data that

00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:11.850
have the statistical
properties of confidential data

00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:14.850
and can be used for certain
types of valid analyses

00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:17.430
but have much lower
confidentiality concerns

00:01:17.430 --> 00:01:19.020
because the data are fake.

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:23.470
You can think of this as
deep fakes for tax research.

00:01:23.470 --> 00:01:25.180
In this talk, I'm
going to try and make

00:01:25.180 --> 00:01:28.060
the case that administrative
data are very useful

00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:30.248
but are difficult to access.

00:01:30.248 --> 00:01:32.290
I'm going to share how
we're working with the IRS

00:01:32.290 --> 00:01:34.690
to safely expand access
to administrative data

00:01:34.690 --> 00:01:35.875
for research.

00:01:35.875 --> 00:01:37.750
And I'm also going to
talk about an R package

00:01:37.750 --> 00:01:42.650
that we're developing that makes
it easier to synthesize data.

00:01:42.650 --> 00:01:46.720
Administrative data are useful,
and I'll give you two examples.

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:50.020
The first example comes
from March of 2020.

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:51.550
It was a scary
time when there was

00:01:51.550 --> 00:01:54.430
a health crisis and an
economic crisis unfolding

00:01:54.430 --> 00:01:56.300
at the same time.

00:01:56.300 --> 00:01:59.600
One issue, though, is that a lot
of our indicators for the labor

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:02.090
market are backward looking.

00:02:02.090 --> 00:02:04.940
And so there's one
indicator, one number

00:02:04.940 --> 00:02:06.680
that came from the
administrative data

00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:08.419
that I think painted
an excellent picture

00:02:08.419 --> 00:02:09.763
of the labor market.

00:02:09.763 --> 00:02:11.180
Many people had
never heard of it,

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:13.640
but it ended up in a
dramatic data visualization

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:15.740
on the cover of
the New York Times.

00:02:15.740 --> 00:02:18.680
This is initial weekly
unemployment insurance claims.

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:21.920
If you look at the bottom
left, that starts in 2000

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:25.407
and it bounces about
from 2000 to 2020.

00:02:25.407 --> 00:02:26.990
And then there's
this black swan event

00:02:26.990 --> 00:02:29.060
that just shows
how scary of a time

00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:31.600
it was in our labor market.

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:33.430
I'll give you another example.

00:02:33.430 --> 00:02:39.460
In 2013, Raj Chetty,
Emmanuel Saez and coauthors

00:02:39.460 --> 00:02:42.220
released groundbreaking
research about intergenerational

00:02:42.220 --> 00:02:44.950
mobility in the United
States, using data

00:02:44.950 --> 00:02:48.450
from 40 million
anonymized tax records.

00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:50.550
This was really
big research that

00:02:50.550 --> 00:02:52.660
deals with how Americans
see themselves.

00:02:52.660 --> 00:02:54.480
Right? We have that
desire for our children

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:56.670
to be better off than ourselves.

00:02:56.670 --> 00:02:58.260
And one of the things
that they found

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:00.810
is that place
matters quite a bit.

00:03:00.810 --> 00:03:03.510
So, for example, the
probability that a child reaches

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:06.450
the top quintile of the
national income distribution,

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:09.000
starting from a family
in the bottom quintile,

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:12.150
is 4.4% in Charlotte,
North Carolina,

00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:15.342
but 12.9% in San
Jose, California,

00:03:15.342 --> 00:03:17.050
I think I said Charlotte,
North Carolina.

00:03:17.050 --> 00:03:18.520
San Jose, California.

00:03:18.520 --> 00:03:21.220
So, really big deal.

00:03:21.220 --> 00:03:23.650
But some people say
that this research never

00:03:23.650 --> 00:03:26.290
should have happened.
In a traditional model

00:03:26.290 --> 00:03:27.770
with an agency like the IRS,

00:03:27.770 --> 00:03:29.520
you do correspondence research.

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:31.830
You have to get your
analysis plan approved.

00:03:31.830 --> 00:03:33.950
You may not actually
see the data.

00:03:33.950 --> 00:03:35.170
You send off your code.

00:03:35.170 --> 00:03:36.170
They run it on the data.

00:03:36.170 --> 00:03:39.350
They review the results
and the like.

00:03:39.350 --> 00:03:42.120
What Saez and Chetty did
was radical and different.

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:45.800
They actually became
employees of the IRS.

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:48.200
They had to go through an
extensive background check,

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:51.620
have their fingerprints taken,
go through lots of training,

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:53.263
have all their results reviewed.

00:03:53.263 --> 00:03:55.430
I've been through this
process, and let me tell you,

00:03:55.430 --> 00:03:58.110
it's not pleasant at all.

00:03:58.110 --> 00:04:00.300
So the question
is, how can we get

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:02.640
this understanding,
this value, this meaning

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:04.680
from administrative data

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:07.710
without all this
inconvenience, all while

00:04:07.710 --> 00:04:09.870
protecting confidentiality?

00:04:09.870 --> 00:04:12.630
And that's why we're
working with synthetic data.

00:04:12.630 --> 00:04:14.610
If we can create
fake data that match

00:04:14.610 --> 00:04:18.029
the statistical properties of
the confidential data, ways

00:04:18.029 --> 00:04:20.459
of safely validating the
results from the synthetic data

00:04:20.459 --> 00:04:22.440
against the confidential
data, then we

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:26.400
can conveniently unlock
a lot of this potential.

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:29.460
So we've worked with the IRS
to create two kind of 1 and 1/2

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:32.340
data sets, the second one still
a bit of a work in progress.

00:04:32.340 --> 00:04:35.070
The first one is called the
supplemental SynPUF, PUF

00:04:35.070 --> 00:04:36.630
for Public Use File.

00:04:36.630 --> 00:04:38.130
And it's a collection
of information

00:04:38.130 --> 00:04:42.310
that the IRS has
about individuals who

00:04:42.310 --> 00:04:45.160
don't, have not filed taxes
and do not have an obligation

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:46.970
to file taxes.

00:04:46.970 --> 00:04:48.710
It's a novel data
set that's going

00:04:48.710 --> 00:04:50.480
to be very helpful
for understanding

00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:52.995
the very low parts of
the income distribution.

00:04:52.995 --> 00:04:54.620
And the tax economists
that I work with

00:04:54.620 --> 00:04:57.010
are pretty excited about it.

00:04:57.010 --> 00:04:59.440
Now you may be asking
yourself, the IRS,

00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:01.757
what are all these
non-filers doing in here?

00:05:01.757 --> 00:05:03.340
What about information
that's gathered

00:05:03.340 --> 00:05:06.490
at places that maybe have one
of these out in front of them?

00:05:06.490 --> 00:05:08.470
And that's the second
file that we're creating.

00:05:08.470 --> 00:05:10.120
It's called the
SynPUF, and it's

00:05:10.120 --> 00:05:13.180
representative of all
taxpayers in the United States.

00:05:13.180 --> 00:05:14.680
Now, the first file
that I mentioned

00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:16.480
had about 20 variables.

00:05:16.480 --> 00:05:18.820
This file has more
than 200 variables,

00:05:18.820 --> 00:05:20.720
hundreds of thousands
of observations.

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:22.390
And it comes from
a complex survey

00:05:22.390 --> 00:05:25.370
with 25 different strata.

00:05:25.370 --> 00:05:27.710
Let's consider a much
simpler example for now,

00:05:27.710 --> 00:05:30.980
where we have four variables
and two observations.

00:05:30.980 --> 00:05:32.880
So that's our
confidential data set.

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:34.790
And the setup of what
we're trying to do

00:05:34.790 --> 00:05:37.460
is create a new data set that
has the exact same record

00:05:37.460 --> 00:05:38.940
layout.

00:05:38.940 --> 00:05:41.040
We're going to employ
a sequential approach

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:42.442
to create this data set.

00:05:42.442 --> 00:05:44.400
So the first thing we'll
do is we'll sample sex

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:47.670
with replacement, then we'll
create a predictive model

00:05:47.670 --> 00:05:50.490
to predict age
conditional on sex.

00:05:50.490 --> 00:05:52.170
Then we'll create
a predictive model

00:05:52.170 --> 00:05:54.610
to predict wages,
conditional on sex and age.

00:05:54.610 --> 00:05:57.210
And finally, a predictive
model to predict

00:05:57.210 --> 00:06:00.450
taxes, conditional on
sex, age and wages.

00:06:00.450 --> 00:06:02.540
A lot of predictive models.

00:06:02.540 --> 00:06:04.502
And this is where I get
to express my gratitude

00:06:04.502 --> 00:06:06.960
for tidymodels and all the
people who have worked on this.

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:09.260
We've heard some great
things about it this morning.

00:06:09.260 --> 00:06:11.120
Tidymodels is a
comprehensive framework

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:14.450
for doing predictive modeling
in R, and we use it extensively

00:06:14.450 --> 00:06:16.630
and in particular, we
use parsnip quite a bit,

00:06:16.630 --> 00:06:18.380
which is an interface
to different machine

00:06:18.380 --> 00:06:21.050
learning and predictive
models, and recipes, which

00:06:21.050 --> 00:06:24.682
is a really powerful package for
feature and target engineering.

00:06:24.682 --> 00:06:26.390
Now in the example
that we're working on,

00:06:26.390 --> 00:06:28.652
right, we need to do
three predictive models.

00:06:28.652 --> 00:06:30.110
I gave you another
example where we

00:06:30.110 --> 00:06:32.610
need to do more than
200 predictive models.

00:06:32.610 --> 00:06:34.280
So we could imagine
having an R script

00:06:34.280 --> 00:06:36.740
where it's model one
predict, model two predict,

00:06:36.740 --> 00:06:38.528
model three predict.

00:06:38.528 --> 00:06:40.820
But if you're going to go
All the way to 200 variables,

00:06:40.820 --> 00:06:44.900
that's going to become a
real pain really quickly.

00:06:44.900 --> 00:06:46.730
So what tidysynthesis
allows us to do

00:06:46.730 --> 00:06:50.240
is actually specify a whole
sequence of predictive models

00:06:50.240 --> 00:06:52.480
for generating synthetic data.

00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:55.000
It also allows us to do a
few things very specific

00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:58.490
to tax or to data synthesis.

00:06:58.490 --> 00:07:00.650
One is it allows us to
add additional noise

00:07:00.650 --> 00:07:04.100
to predicted values for extra
confidentiality protection.

00:07:04.100 --> 00:07:06.820
It allows us to specify
constraints in the data.

00:07:06.820 --> 00:07:08.840
Things like individual
income can't

00:07:08.840 --> 00:07:10.760
exceed family income
or interest income

00:07:10.760 --> 00:07:14.100
can't exceed your
personal income.

00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:16.660
It also allows us to
predict from our models

00:07:16.660 --> 00:07:17.410
in different ways.

00:07:17.410 --> 00:07:19.790
And I'll talk about
that more in a second.

00:07:19.790 --> 00:07:21.480
So simply put, tidysynthesis.

00:07:21.480 --> 00:07:23.660
The goal is all the
power of tidymodels

00:07:23.660 --> 00:07:28.900
for data synthesis concisely
with a few special tools.

00:07:28.900 --> 00:07:30.478
So what does this
actually look like?

00:07:30.478 --> 00:07:32.020
I'm going to show
you how we're going

00:07:32.020 --> 00:07:34.370
to synthesize a data set today.

00:07:34.370 --> 00:07:37.550
I'm not going to synthesize
unemployment insurance data.

00:07:37.550 --> 00:07:38.810
To the relief of many of you,

00:07:38.810 --> 00:07:41.230
I'm not going to
synthesize tax data.

00:07:41.230 --> 00:07:44.800
We could imagine synthesizing
electronic health records.

00:07:44.800 --> 00:07:46.330
Or maybe we're a
business and we're

00:07:46.330 --> 00:07:48.730
interested in unlocking
information from our customer

00:07:48.730 --> 00:07:51.220
data without sacrificing
the confidentiality

00:07:51.220 --> 00:07:52.770
of any individual in our data.

00:07:52.770 --> 00:07:54.490
But I'm not going
to do that either.

00:07:54.490 --> 00:07:57.580
No instead, we're going to
synthesize penguins data

00:07:57.580 --> 00:08:00.040
because penguins
deserve privacy, too.

00:08:00.040 --> 00:08:02.600
So we're going to synthesize
the palmerenguins data set.

00:08:02.600 --> 00:08:06.200
We already had a good
introduction to this earlier.

00:08:06.200 --> 00:08:08.690
Right? So we have information
about species and island

00:08:08.690 --> 00:08:11.487
and sex and these numeric
measures, measurements

00:08:11.487 --> 00:08:12.570
of the different penguins.

00:08:12.570 --> 00:08:15.028
And we're really going to focus
on these numeric variables.

00:08:15.028 --> 00:08:17.150
And our goal is to create
synthetic data that

00:08:17.150 --> 00:08:20.780
match the layout of
that confidential data.

00:08:20.780 --> 00:08:23.450
So we can start asking
ourselves some questions

00:08:23.450 --> 00:08:24.810
of what this should look like.

00:08:24.810 --> 00:08:28.560
If you have 200 variables,
what order should we use?

00:08:28.560 --> 00:08:30.380
Well, tidysynthesis
has a function

00:08:30.380 --> 00:08:32.480
that employs different
heuristics that you can use

00:08:32.480 --> 00:08:34.562
to determine synthesis order.

00:08:34.562 --> 00:08:36.770
In this case, I'm going to
use my extensive knowledge

00:08:36.770 --> 00:08:38.630
of penguins, which
is absolutely none

00:08:38.630 --> 00:08:41.429
to determine that bill length
is a very important variable.

00:08:41.429 --> 00:08:43.429
And then we're going to
synthesize the remaining

00:08:43.429 --> 00:08:46.340
variables in order from most
correlated with bill length

00:08:46.340 --> 00:08:47.810
to least correlated.

00:08:47.810 --> 00:08:49.850
Once we create this
visit sequence object,

00:08:49.850 --> 00:08:53.750
it's going to be reused in
a lot of other functions.

00:08:53.750 --> 00:08:57.080
What kind of algorithms should
we use for our predictive models?

00:08:57.080 --> 00:08:59.360
In this case, we're going
to use a regression tree,

00:08:59.360 --> 00:09:02.300
and this is where parsnip
really shines in tidysynthesis.

00:09:02.300 --> 00:09:04.550
So we just specify
regression tree implemented

00:09:04.550 --> 00:09:05.530
with the rpart engine.

00:09:08.120 --> 00:09:10.710
Now we need to specify that for
all of our different models.

00:09:10.710 --> 00:09:12.793
And this is where we're
really saving on all that

00:09:12.793 --> 00:09:14.750
R code. In this
case, we just specify

00:09:14.750 --> 00:09:17.810
that we want to use the rpart
mod for every single variable.

00:09:17.810 --> 00:09:19.825
There's three ways
to specify this.

00:09:19.825 --> 00:09:21.200
The first one is
like this, where

00:09:21.200 --> 00:09:23.720
we use the same model
for all variables.

00:09:23.720 --> 00:09:25.790
Alternatively, we
can specify a default

00:09:25.790 --> 00:09:27.920
and then override it
for specific variables,

00:09:27.920 --> 00:09:29.630
or we can specify a
different model

00:09:29.630 --> 00:09:32.470
for every single variable.

00:09:32.470 --> 00:09:35.530
What types of feature or target
engineering should we use?

00:09:35.530 --> 00:09:38.080
In this case, this is where
recipes really shines in

00:09:38.080 --> 00:09:39.190
tidysynthesis.

00:09:39.190 --> 00:09:43.523
We can specify any set of
steps in recipes for our synthesis.

00:09:43.523 --> 00:09:45.940
In this case, we're not going
to use any feature or target

00:09:45.940 --> 00:09:46.670
engineering.

00:09:46.670 --> 00:09:48.545
We're just going to use
this helper function,

00:09:48.545 --> 00:09:53.533
construct recipes, basically
construct a series of formula.

00:09:53.533 --> 00:09:54.950
Finally, how are
we actually going

00:09:54.950 --> 00:09:57.560
to come up with synthetic
data from our models?

00:09:57.560 --> 00:09:59.390
Now, if you just use
the predict function,

00:09:59.390 --> 00:10:03.030
oftentimes you get a conditional
mean or a conditional median.

00:10:03.030 --> 00:10:05.425
This actually makes for
really bad synthetic data

00:10:05.425 --> 00:10:06.800
because the data
sets that you're

00:10:06.800 --> 00:10:09.130
creating don't have adequate
sample variance, right?

00:10:09.130 --> 00:10:11.720
You don't have enough variation
in the resulting data.

00:10:11.720 --> 00:10:14.330
So in this case, we're going to
use a function called sample R

00:10:14.330 --> 00:10:14.960
part.

00:10:14.960 --> 00:10:16.850
If you're familiar
with regression trees,

00:10:16.850 --> 00:10:19.250
you navigate to your final
node and oftentimes you

00:10:19.250 --> 00:10:20.760
might take the
mean of that node.

00:10:20.760 --> 00:10:23.180
Instead, we're going to
sample from that final node

00:10:23.180 --> 00:10:27.570
to get this prediction
distribution.

00:10:27.570 --> 00:10:29.817
So I just walked you through
two of the functions.

00:10:29.817 --> 00:10:31.650
There's a lot of other
functions and objects

00:10:31.650 --> 00:10:34.770
in tidysynthesis that can
control the synthesis process.

00:10:34.770 --> 00:10:36.720
There's noise for
adding additional noise

00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:38.160
to predicted values.

00:10:38.160 --> 00:10:40.200
There's constraints
for elegantly enforcing

00:10:40.200 --> 00:10:41.418
constraints in the data.

00:10:41.418 --> 00:10:42.960
And there's replicates
if we actually

00:10:42.960 --> 00:10:45.290
want to synthesize more
than one data set, which

00:10:45.290 --> 00:10:46.415
can be handled in parallel.

00:10:49.390 --> 00:10:51.710
Tidymodels is lazy, right?

00:10:51.710 --> 00:10:54.790
You create a bunch of objects,
but a lot of the computation

00:10:54.790 --> 00:10:57.040
doesn't actually happen
until you use like a tune

00:10:57.040 --> 00:10:58.970
function or a fit function.

00:10:58.970 --> 00:11:01.390
And tidysynthesis is
the exact same way.

00:11:01.390 --> 00:11:04.180
So much like you have a
workflow in tidymodels,

00:11:04.180 --> 00:11:07.420
we have a presynth object where
we bring together everything

00:11:07.420 --> 00:11:09.197
and then the actual
hard work starts

00:11:09.197 --> 00:11:10.780
when you run the
synthesized function.

00:11:10.780 --> 00:11:12.910
This is where you go
check Twitter or get

00:11:12.910 --> 00:11:14.090
your cup of coffee.

00:11:14.090 --> 00:11:18.017
If you have 200 variables,
maybe let it run overnight.

00:11:18.017 --> 00:11:19.850
So I promise that we
are going to synthesize

00:11:19.850 --> 00:11:20.760
some penguins data.

00:11:20.760 --> 00:11:23.397
And I have a quick
video to show this off.

00:11:23.397 --> 00:11:23.980
In the right,

00:11:23.980 --> 00:11:26.580
you'll notice we have
about 300 observations.

00:11:26.580 --> 00:11:28.230
If we wrote all
this code out, it

00:11:28.230 --> 00:11:30.100
might be a few hundred 
lines of code.

00:11:30.100 --> 00:11:33.143
But in this case, it's only
about 70 lines of code.

00:11:33.143 --> 00:11:35.310
I'm going to highlight
everything and click Run here

00:11:35.310 --> 00:11:37.477
in a second and you'll see
that it quickly gets down

00:11:37.477 --> 00:11:38.797
to the synthesize function.

00:11:38.797 --> 00:11:41.130
Now, if you've worked with
the Palmer penguin's data set

00:11:41.130 --> 00:11:43.140
before, I want you
to close your eyes

00:11:43.140 --> 00:11:45.390
and imagine what that
data set looks like.

00:11:45.390 --> 00:11:49.020
This will take about 10
to 12 seconds to run.

00:11:49.020 --> 00:11:50.880
And now open your
eyes, unless you

00:11:50.880 --> 00:11:52.120
have it perfectly memorized,

00:11:52.120 --> 00:11:54.930
hopefully this looks like what
you were seeing in your mind,

00:11:54.930 --> 00:11:55.950
right?

00:11:55.950 --> 00:11:57.690
This looks like the
penguins data set,

00:11:57.690 --> 00:11:58.960
but it's entirely made up.

00:11:58.960 --> 00:12:01.430
It's entirely fake.

00:12:01.430 --> 00:12:03.060
So we have synthetic data.

00:12:03.060 --> 00:12:04.280
We're done, right?

00:12:04.280 --> 00:12:06.620
If this is all I had to
do, I'd be very happy.

00:12:06.620 --> 00:12:08.270
I'd spend lots of
time by the pool

00:12:08.270 --> 00:12:11.000
with a drink and the little
umbrellas and everything

00:12:11.000 --> 00:12:12.140
relaxing.

00:12:12.140 --> 00:12:14.270
But in reality, I spend
most of my day saying,

00:12:14.270 --> 00:12:16.840
have we done a good enough job?

00:12:16.840 --> 00:12:19.430
You can imagine looking at
this in a few different ways.

00:12:19.430 --> 00:12:23.500
I mean, on the surface, it looks
like the penguins data set.

00:12:23.500 --> 00:12:25.960
We can look at the univariate
distributions for the four

00:12:25.960 --> 00:12:27.640
numeric variables.

00:12:27.640 --> 00:12:29.380
Here I have density
plots for them

00:12:29.380 --> 00:12:31.210
where the synthetic
data are in blue

00:12:31.210 --> 00:12:33.020
and the confidential
data are in yellow.

00:12:33.020 --> 00:12:34.450
And you see that
we pretty closely

00:12:34.450 --> 00:12:37.090
recreate the univariate
distributions for each of them.

00:12:37.090 --> 00:12:40.040
And in fact, we only
have 300 observations.

00:12:40.040 --> 00:12:41.560
So any differences
here could just

00:12:41.560 --> 00:12:44.490
be because of sampling error.

00:12:44.490 --> 00:12:47.120
We can also look at some
bivariate relationships.

00:12:47.120 --> 00:12:49.620
Here I have the relationship
between flipper length and bill

00:12:49.620 --> 00:12:52.560
length, flipper length and
bill depth, and flipper length

00:12:52.560 --> 00:12:53.710
and body mass.

00:12:53.710 --> 00:12:55.350
Again, the synthetic
data are in blue

00:12:55.350 --> 00:12:57.120
and the confidential
data are in yellow.

00:12:57.120 --> 00:12:59.880
And in all three cases, I
think we do a pretty good job

00:12:59.880 --> 00:13:01.740
of recreating the data.

00:13:01.740 --> 00:13:04.140
And in fact, if you
fit regression lines,

00:13:04.140 --> 00:13:08.860
which I've done here, they
look almost identical.

00:13:08.860 --> 00:13:11.230
But maybe we're not satisfied.

00:13:11.230 --> 00:13:13.450
Maybe we want to
specify a new synthesis

00:13:13.450 --> 00:13:14.930
and start all over again.

00:13:14.930 --> 00:13:17.300
The good news is we're not
starting from the beginning.

00:13:17.300 --> 00:13:20.200
Just like how in tidymodels
you can reuse objects sort of

00:13:20.200 --> 00:13:21.630
over and over and over again.

00:13:21.630 --> 00:13:24.490
Here we can re-use all of
our objects from earlier,

00:13:24.490 --> 00:13:26.620
add some information
about adding noise

00:13:26.620 --> 00:13:27.800
to our predicted values.

00:13:27.800 --> 00:13:30.170
Add some constraints
and synthesize.

00:13:33.260 --> 00:13:35.900
So just as a recap,
administrative data

00:13:35.900 --> 00:13:38.000
are useful but
difficult to access,

00:13:38.000 --> 00:13:39.823
and I think there's
a huge potential

00:13:39.823 --> 00:13:41.240
to understand more
about our world

00:13:41.240 --> 00:13:44.750
if we can make it easier
to access those data.

00:13:44.750 --> 00:13:46.080
As a proof of concept,

00:13:46.080 --> 00:13:47.900
we've created two
fully synthetic data

00:13:47.900 --> 00:13:50.510
sets with the IRS,
and we've built

00:13:50.510 --> 00:13:52.610
this package called
tidysynthesis so

00:13:52.610 --> 00:13:56.990
that other agencies, nonprofits
and businesses can implement

00:13:56.990 --> 00:13:59.750
these methods without
having to write thousands

00:13:59.750 --> 00:14:01.980
of lines of code.

00:14:01.980 --> 00:14:04.770
Now, the data privacy community,
the synthetic data community

00:14:04.770 --> 00:14:07.100
are pretty small,
and especially they

00:14:07.100 --> 00:14:08.850
don't have that many
statisticians or data

00:14:08.850 --> 00:14:10.640
scientists.

00:14:10.640 --> 00:14:13.040
So if this is something that
gets you excited, if you're

00:14:13.040 --> 00:14:16.100
interested in joining the
raft of privacy penguins,

00:14:16.100 --> 00:14:18.740
please don't hesitate to reach
out to me after this talk here

00:14:18.740 --> 00:14:22.640
in person, on Twitter, at
awunderground or on GitHub.

00:14:22.640 --> 00:14:24.200
Maybe this could be you.

00:14:24.200 --> 00:14:26.080
Thank you.

